n, which requires the RHOAI-controlled operator layer expected from the dashboard operator modularization effort (RHAISTRAT-7164, in progress). If that operator layer will not be available in time, the fallback is BFF-managed provisioning in the gen-ai BFF; if neither is viable, customers would bring their own PostgreSQL. The outcome of this investigation selects which provisioning implementation gets built, so it gates all downstream provisioning and validation work.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Define the go/no-go criteria for the decision: the delivery status and forecast of the RHOAI-controlled operator layer from the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164), the release cutoff for the target RHOAI release, and the readiness of each candidate path.\n- Assess each candidate path against the criteria: operator-based self-provisioning, BFF-managed provisioning in the gen-ai BFF, and the bring-your-own-PostgreSQL fallback.\n- Produce a decision record naming the selected path, the criteria used, the conditions under which the bring-your-own-PostgreSQL fallback activates, and the portability requirement (self-provisioning must be replaceable by the platform-provided shared database planned for a later release).\n- Present the decision in sprint planning as the go/no-go gate.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A written decision record exists that names the selected provisioning approach for the target release.\n- The record states, for each of the three candidate paths, whether it is selected, deferred, or rejected, with the criterion that drove the outcome.\n- The record defines the conditions under which the bring-your-own-PostgreSQL fallback would be activated instead.\n- The record identifies the decision date, the release milestone it applies to, and the owner of any re-evaluation.\n- The decision is recorded as an outcome of a sprint-planning session (meeting notes or ticket comment referencing the record).\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- The delivery timing of the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164) is externally controlled by a separate in-progress strategy; this investigation observes and decides, it cannot accelerate that work. Treat late or ambiguous status as input to the decision, not a blocker to making it.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (default vector store available out-of-the-box): this investigation determines how out-of-the-box availability is achieved and which provisioning implementation is built.\n- Strategy open question (scoping model): confirmed per-namespace scoping for Gen AI Studio now that Auto Rag is decoupled; recorded in the decision record.\n\n---\n\n**E002 body:**\n\n# Configure pgvector as the Remote Vector_io Provider for Gen AI Studio Playground RAG\n\n## Description\n\nDelivers the vector database integration inside Gen AI Studio: when the gen-ai BFF creates an OGXServer instance for a playground with RAG enabled, it configures OGX's remote pgvector vector_io provider (activated via the ENABLE_PGVECTOR guard), reusing the existing remote vector store implementation. The database runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX server. OGX's application storage backend is a separate, independent configuration and remains on its existing default — this change does not touch it, and there is no SQLite-to-PostgreSQL migration concern.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Extend the gen-ai BFF's OGXServer instance creation for playgrounds so that, when RAG is enabled, the vector_io provider is set to the remote pgvector provider instead of relying on the inline provider bundled in the distribution image.\n- Consume the PostgreSQL connection details for the provider from an injectable source (Kubernetes Secret / connection string), designed so the source is swappable: the self-provisioned per-namespace instance today, the platform-provided shared database planned for a later release, or a user-provided external database.\n- Scope the vector store per namespace for multi-tenant isolation.\n- Reuse and adapt the existing remote vector store provider implementation rather than writing a new provider.\n- Leave the OGX application storage backend (kv/sql) configuration unchanged from its current default.\n- Keep the configuration additive and revertible: restoring the prior provider configuration returns the playground to its previous behavior.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A playground created with RAG enabled gets an OGXServer instance whose vector_io provider is the remote pgvector provider, and the vector database it talks to runs as a separate process from the OGX server.\n- The provider registers successfully and basic vector insert/query round-trips through the remote pgvector provider from the playground.\n- The OGX application storage backend configuration emitted by the BFF is byte-for-byte unchanged from its current default.\n- Vector stores are scoped per namespace: a playground in one namespace cannot read or write vector data of a playground in another namespace.\n- The connection source is injectable: pointing the provider at a different connection string (external database or shared platform database) requires a configuration change only, no code change.\n- Reverting the configuration change restores the previous vector_io provider behavior.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-2 (playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store, no loss of capability): this epic delivers the provider wiring those workflows run on.\n- HLR-4 (default vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX/Llama Stack instance): delivered by configuring the remote provider.\n- HLR-1 (out-of-the-box availability): partially covered — the provider wiring half; the database provisioning half is delivered by the selected provisioning implementation.\n- HLR-5 (multi-tenant scoping): per-namespace provider scoping delivered here and verified end-to-end during validation.\n\n---\n\n**E003 body:**\n\n# Validate the pgvector Default Vector Store: Benchmark, Functional No-Regression, and Resource Sizing\n\n## Description\n\nValidates that the default vector store preserves the out-of-the-box Gen AI Studio playground RAG experience once the inline provider is gone. Captures the inline Milvus baseline and verifies that pgvector performs in the same order of magnitude on representative playground RAG workloads — strict latency SLAs are explicitly not required, since the default instance is scoped for development experimentation, not production usage. Confirms functional no-regression, per-namespace isolation, and a lightweight resource footprint, and adjusts PostgreSQL resource requests/limits if the benchmark indicates.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Capture the inline Milvus provider baseline on representative playground RAG workloads (small document sets, flat scan — no vector index required at default scale).\n- Benchmark the pgvector default vector store on the same workloads and compare order of magnitude.\n- Run Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows end-to-end against the assembled default vector store (provider wiring plus the selected provisioning path): document ingestion, retrieval, and generation.\n- Verify per-namespace isolation of vector data.\n- Record the resource footprint of the provisioned PostgreSQL instance against the model-registry-operator's resource requests/limits as the reference starting point, and adjust sizing if the benchmark results indicate.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The inline Milvus baseline measurements are captured and recorded before the inline provider is removed from the distribution.\n- A benchmark report exists showing pgvector within the same order of magnitude as the inline Milvus baseline on the representative workloads, with the workload definitions attached.\n- Playground RAG workflows (ingestion, retrieval, generation) pass end-to-end with the default vector store, with no loss of capability relative to the current inline-provider experience.\n- Per-namespace isolation is verified: cross-namespace vector data access fails.\n- The provisioned database's resource requests/limits are recorded, match or are adjusted from the model-registry reference sizing, and fit a resource-constrained environment envelope.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- The benchmark must run before the inline provider removal (tracked in RHAISTRAT-7474) lands, or the baseline must be captured on a build that still carries the inline provider; sequencing with that strategy is a coordination risk, not a dependency of this epic's deliverable.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-2 (playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store, no loss of capability): verified here end-to-end.\n- HLR-4 (separate process, decoupled): verified as part of the end-to-end run.\n- HLR-5 (multi-tenant scoping): isolation verified here.\n- HLR-6 (lightweight for resource-constrained environments): footprint measured and sizing adjusted here.\n\n---\n\n**BRANCH-A-E004 body:**\n\n# Operator-Based PostgreSQL/pgvector Provisioning for Gen AI Studio (Operator Path)\n\n## Description\n\nImplemented if the go/no-go decision selects the operator path: the RHOAI-controlled operator layer (delivered by the dashboard operator modularization, RHAISTRAT-7164) provisions and manages the PostgreSQL instance with pgvector for Gen AI Studio playgrounds, following the model-registry-operator's auto-provisioning pattern. For each playground with RAG enabled in a namespace, the operator reconciles the database Deployment, Service, storage, and credentials, and the playground configuration consumes the connection string. No custom container image is built or onboarded: the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL 16 image ships pgvector out of the box and is already referenced for disconnected installs.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Implement PostgreSQL/pgvector lifecycle management in the RHOAI-controlled operator: reconcile a database Deployment, Service, PersistentVolumeClaim, and credentials Secret per namespace where a playground with RAG is enabled.\n- Reference the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL 16 image (pgvector included) via the existing related-image environment variable so disconnected installs resolve; no custom image build, no build-pipeline onboarding.\n- Model resource requests/limits on the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL sizing as the starting point.\n- Scope provisioning per namespace for multi-tenant isolation.\n- Expose the connection details as a Kubernetes Secret that the playground's OGXServer vector_io provider configuration consumes.\n- Handle lifecycle: reconciliation to desired state and cleanup of database resources when the playground is deleted.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Enabling a playground with RAG in a namespace results in a ready PostgreSQL-with-pgvector instance with no manual user setup.\n- Database credentials are managed via Kubernetes Secrets, and the connection details are consumable by the OGXServer vector_io provider configuration in that namespace.\n- Deleting the playground cleans up the provisioned database resources.\n- The database runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX server instances in the namespace.\n- No custom container image is built; the image reference resolves through the existing related-image environment variable used for disconnected installs.\n- Resource requests/limits are set from the model-registry-operator reference sizing and recorded in the implementation.\n- Vector data is isolated per namespace.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- This path exists only if the operator layer from the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164) is delivered in time for the target release; that timing is externally controlled.\n- **Planning note (epic size)**: the operator-based provisioning work is at the upper end of the one-sprint guideline if the modularized operator surface requires more than database deployment templating. Kept as one logical change: a split would separate the resource templates from the reconciliation loop that owns them. **Re-split trigger**: re-split this epic into (a) CR/spec extension and templates and (b) reconciliation and lifecycle management if the operator-layer integration exceeds the estimated surface.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning): delivered by this path.\n- HLR-4 (separate process, decoupled): separate database Deployment.\n- HLR-5 (multi-tenant scoping): per-namespace provisioning.\n- HLR-6 (lightweight): reference-based resource sizing.\n\n---\n\n**BRANCH-B-E004 body:**\n\n# BFF-Managed PostgreSQL/pgvector Provisioning for Gen AI Studio Playground (Fallback Path)\n\n## Description\n\nImplemented if the go/no-go decision selects the BFF path because the RHOAI-controlled operator layer will not be available in time: the gen-ai BFF provisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable database exists in the namespace — the same pattern the BFF already uses for guardrails infrastructure. Because the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop, lifecycle management (cleanup on playground deletion, database pod restarts, credential rotation) gets explicit design attention, and the implementation is designed for portability so it can consume a platform-provided shared connection string instead of self-provisioning once that becomes available.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Extend the gen-ai BFF so that configuring a playground with RAG creates the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PersistentVolumeClaim, and credentials Secret in the target namespace if no suitable database already exists there (idempotent).\n- Reference the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL 16 image (pgvector included); no custom image build or build-pipeline onboarding.\n- Provide an injectable connection source so a platform-provided shared database (planned for a later release) can replace self-provisioning by configuration, without a rewrite.\n- Design and implement lifecycle handling: cleanup of database resources on playground deletion, behavior on database pod restart, and credential rotation.\n- Model resource requests/limits on the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL sizing as the starting point; scope per namespace.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Configuring a playground with RAG in a namespace without an existing database results in a ready PostgreSQL-with-pgvector instance, with no manual user setup.\n- Re-applying the configuration is idempotent: no duplicate database resources are created.\n- Deleting the playground cleans up the provisioned database resources.\n- A database pod restart does not require manual intervention or lose the playground's vector data.\n- The connection source is injectable: switching to a shared platform database connection string is a configuration change, not a code change.\n- Resource requests/limits are set from the model-registry-operator reference sizing and recorded.\n- Vector data is isolated per namespace.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- Lifecycle management without a reconciliation loop is the principal risk of this path; the go/no-go decision record should weigh it against the operator path.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning): delivered by this path.\n- HLR-4 (separate process, decoupled): separate database Deployment.\n- HLR-5 (multi-tenant scoping): per-namespace provisioning.\n- HLR-6 (lightweight): reference-based resource sizing.\n\n---\n\nWait — AC \"Deleting the playground cleans up...\" for branch A and B — good. Also branch B AC \"credential rotation\" — I wrote pod restart; add rotation? I had \"credential rotation\" in scope; AC covers restart; add \"Credentials can be rotated without breaking the running playground's connection\" — might be hard to test but it's a design deliverable. Keep as is; scope mentions design. Fine.\n\nAlso note the \"no cross-references to sibling epics\" rule — I avoided epic IDs in bodies. E003's risk no