2 ↔ BRANCH-A/B-E004 | Rule 13 (configuration-reference exception) | The PostgreSQL connection string the provider config consumes is a configuration reference filled in at execution time, not a build-against artifact. Expressed as the injectable-connection-source acceptance criterion on E002, never as a DAG edge. |\n| No edge BRANCH-*-E004 → E003 | Branch rule (main-plan epics never depend on branch epics) | E003 depends on the investigation (E001) whose outcome selects the branch; in execution, validation follows the selected branch's implementation. |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\nHLRs are derived from the strategy's Acceptance Criteria (the HLR table lives in the unavailable strategy attachment). All are P0 per the strategy's Blocker priority — there are no P1/P2 HLRs, so no priority-split analysis applies and no lower-priority HLR is folded into a higher-priority epic.\n\n| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic(s) | Notes |\n|-----|----------|-------------|---------|-------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | A default vector store is available out-of-the-box without manual provisioning or configuration of external infrastructure | RHAISTRAT-8004-E002 (provider half); RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E004 or RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004 (provisioning half — exactly one, per go/no-go outcome) | Provisioning approach selected by E001 |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | Gen AI Studio playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store with no loss of current capability | RHAISTRAT-8004-E002, RHAISTRAT-8004-E003 | |\n| HLR-3 | P0 | Auto Rag functions with the default vector store with no loss of current capability | — (removed from scope) | Removed per Staff Engineer Input: Auto Rag compatibility with pgvector is RHAISTRAT-7824's scope (RHOAI 3.6). Deliberately not covered here; see Health Warnings. |\n| HLR-4 | P0 | The default vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the Llama Stack instance (not an inline provider) | RHAISTRAT-8004-E002; RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E004 / RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004; verified in RHAISTRAT-8004-E003 | |\n| HLR-5 | P0 | The default vector store is scoped appropriately for multi-tenant environments (per-namespace isolation) | RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E004 / RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004; verified in RHAISTRAT-8004-E003 | Per-namespace confirmed: the per-instance concern was Auto Rag's, now decoupled |\n| HLR-6 | P0 | The default vector store is lightweight enough for resource-constrained environments without significant overhead | RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-A-E004 / RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004; measured and adjusted in RHAISTRAT-8004-E003 | Sizing modeled on the model-registry-operator reference |\n\n## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n\nLogged for traceability (Staff Engineer Input takes precedence over the AI-generated technical approach):\n\n1. **Auto Rag removed from scope** (affected components, impacted teams, acceptance criteria, validation requirements) — RHAISTRAT-7824 owns Auto Rag/pgvector compatibility targeting RHOAI 3.6. HLR-3 above reflects this.\n2. **Corrected the PostgreSQL premise**: OGX does not auto-provision PostgreSQL — it defaults to SQLite for application storage, and the gen-ai BFF hardcodes SQLite when creating OGXServer instances. Introducing PostgreSQL for vectors is new work, not an extension of existing infrastructure.\n3. **No custom image build, no Konflux onboarding**: `registry.redhat.io/rhel9/postgresql-16` already includes pgvector, the model-registry-operator already auto-provisions it, and the related-image environment variable is already in the rhods-operator CSV for disconnected installs. The strategy's 1–2 sprint image-onboarding estimate is eliminated — no `repo-onboarding`, `konflux-onboarding`, or `license-validation` epics are generated.\n4. **Vector storage and application storage are independent configurations**: only the pgvector vector_io provider is configured; the SQLite application-storage backend is untouched, and there is no SQLite-to-PostgreSQL migration concern.\n5. **Performance thresholds, corpus-size ranges, and index-type selection removed as open questions**: dev-experimentation scope — order-of-magnitude benchmark against the inline Milvus baseline, flat scan at default scale, no SLA thresholds.\n6. **Platform-provided shared PostgreSQL via DSC arrives no earlier than RHOAI 3.6**: for the current release Gen AI Studio must provision its own instance — hence the go/no-go between operator-based and BFF-managed self-provisioning, with bring-your-own PostgreSQL as the last-resort fallback.\n\n## Component Mapping Notes\n\n- gen-ai BFF work (odh-dashboard repo, `packages/gen-ai`) → canonical component `Gen AI Studio` (the product capability the BFF serves).\n- Operator-layer provisioning (branch A — the RHOAI-controlled operator delivered by the dashboard operator modularization, RHAISTRAT-7164) → canonical component `AI Core Dashboard`.\n- OGX distribution and ogx-k8s-operator are **passive** here (no code changes; keep working): the remote pgvector vector_io provider already exists in the OGX distribution, corroborated by `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/ogx-distribution.md` (PGVector listed as an optional vector DB backend; remote-only provider set after inline-provider stripping; providers activated via environment-variable guards). Their continued function is validated as acceptance criteria on E002/E003 rather than separate epics — the same owning team validates.\n- Rule 22 (infrastructure not in platform inventory) was evaluated for the PostgreSQL image: existence and working precedent are corroborated by `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/model-registry-operator.md` (controller-created `{registry-name}-postgres` Deployments/Services/Secrets), so no separate existence-validation Investigation is generated; functional validation is folded into E003.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy attachment (fidelity)**: the full strategy body (HLR table, Affected Components, Impacted Teams, Dependencies, NFRs) is a Jira attachment not present in this workspace. HLRs were derived from the acceptance criteria and prioritized from the strategy's Blocker priority. If the attachment becomes available, re-check HLR wording and any NFRs (disconnected-install behavior, sizing constraints) against this decomposition.\n- **External timing risk (schedule risk, not an AC)**: the primary provisioning path depends on the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164, in progress), whose delivery timing is outside this strategy's control and cannot be compressed. Recorded as a risk on E001 and branch A — it is an input to the go/no-go decision, never an acceptance condition.\n- **Planning note (epic size)**: the operator-based provisioning epic (branch A) sits at the upper end of the one-sprint guideline if the modularized operator surface requires more than database deployment templating. Kept as one logical change: a split would separate the resource templates from the reconciliation loop that owns them. **Re-split trigger**: re-split branch A into (a) CR/spec extension with resource templates and (b) reconciliation and lifecycle management if the operator-layer integration exceeds the estimated surface.\n- **Scope boundary — Auto Rag**: the strategy title and one acceptance criterion name Auto Rag, but Staff Engineer Input removes it to RHAISTRAT-7824 (RHOAI 3.6). Watch for double coverage between this strategy's validation and RHAISTRAT-7824's scope at review and planning time.\n- **Open question disposition**: the migration-path question (default vector store → production-grade vector database) does not change which epics exist — the pgvector provider sits behind OGX's standard remote vector_io interface, so moving to an external vector database is reconfiguration. Captured as the injectable-connection-source acceptance criterion on E002 rather than a separate epic. The scoping-model question is resolved as per-namespace for Gen AI Studio (the per-instance concern was Auto Rag's, now decoupled).\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nThe external dependency (RHAISTRAT-7164 operator-layer timing) has uncertain timing, and partial value is deliverable without it:\n\n- **Tier 1 (independent, start immediately)**: E002 — the remote pgvector provider wiring is identical under every provisioning outcome; it delivers the vector_io integration and de-risks the reuse assumption while the go/no-go is pending.\n- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated)**: BRANCH-A-E004 / BRANCH-B-E004 — the provisioning implementation selected by the go/no-go decision, which is itself contingent on the RHAISTRAT-7164 delivery forecast. The bring-your-own-PostgreSQL option (users configure the OGXServer CR spec with their own connection details, aligned with OGX 1.0 breaking changes) is the recorded last-resort fallback if neither self-provisioning path is viable.\n- **Closing**: E003 validates the assembled capability (wiring + selected provisioning path) and closes the strategy's no-regression acceptance criteria; RHAISTRAT-7474 (inline provider removal) is unblocked once validation passes.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":75067,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-95f83f4e9296bd43","agent_id":"a9613ec76490a22b1","session_id":"90eb4e85-7690-4532-889d-850d91d0a9f8","uuid":"dc8c29be-9db7-40b0-bb80-6e3a4dd09ffc","timestamp":"2026-10-25T20:39:18.751Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
