{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-800ff8fa-97bb-4f04-af86-7a1f716b0126","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-836b9b5b1e6e2e4b","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-o1xgnpoh/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-E001.md","content":"# Go/No-Go Decision: PostgreSQL Provisioning Approach for the Default Vector Store\n\n## Description\n\nResolves which provisioning approach Gen AI Studio uses to make a PostgreSQL/pgvector instance available out-of-the-box for playground RAG workflows in the target RHOAI release. The primary path is operator-based self-provisioning following the model-registry pattern, which requires the RHOAI-controlled operator layer expected from the dashboard operator modularization effort (RHAISTRAT-7164, in progress). If that operator layer will not be available in time, the fallback is BFF-managed provisioning in the gen-ai BFF; if neither self-provisioning path is viable, customers would bring their own PostgreSQL. The outcome of this investigation selects which provisioning implementation gets built, so it gates all downstream provisioning and validation work.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Define the go/no-go criteria for the decision: the delivery status and forecast of the RHOAI-controlled operator layer from the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164), the release cutoff for the target RHOAI release, and the readiness of each candidate path.\n- Assess each candidate path against the criteria: operator-based self-provisioning, BFF-managed provisioning in the gen-ai BFF, and the bring-your-own-PostgreSQL fallback.\n- Produce a decision record naming the selected path, the criteria used, the conditions under which the bring-your-own-PostgreSQL fallback activates, and the portability requirement (self-provisioning must be replaceable by the platform-provided shared database planned for a later RHOAI release).\n- Present the decision in sprint planning as the go/no-go gate.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A written decision record exists that names the selected provisioning approach for the target release.\n- The record states, for each of the three candidate paths, whether it is selected, deferred, or rejected, with the criterion that drove the outcome.\n- The record defines the conditions under which the bring-your-own-PostgreSQL fallback would be activated instead of self-provisioning.\n- The record identifies the decision date, the release milestone it applies to, and the owner of any re-evaluation.\n- The decision is recorded as an outcome of a sprint-planning session (meeting notes or ticket comment referencing the record).\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- The delivery timing of the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164) is externally controlled by a separate in-progress strategy; this investigation observes that status and decides — it cannot accelerate the underlying work. Late or ambiguous delivery status is an input to the decision, not a blocker to making it.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (default vector store available out-of-the-box): this investigation determines how out-of-the-box availability is achieved and which provisioning implementation is built.\n- Strategy open question (scoping model): per-namespace scoping confirmed for Gen AI Studio now that Auto Rag is decoupled; recorded in the decision record.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":89438,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-95f83f4e9296bd43","agent_id":"a9613ec76490a22b1","session_id":"90eb4e85-7690-4532-889d-850d91d0a9f8","uuid":"fc784800-e782-41a6-a843-32936df3b6de","timestamp":"2026-10-25T20:39:29.916Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8004"}
