{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-800ff8fa-97bb-4f04-af86-7a1f716b0126","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-a4f258b3876b3d00","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8004-o1xgnpoh/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004.md","content":"# BFF-Managed PostgreSQL/pgvector Provisioning for Gen AI Studio Playground (Fallback Path)\n\n## Description\n\nImplemented if the go/no-go decision selects the BFF path because the RHOAI-controlled operator layer will not be available in time for the target release: the gen-ai BFF provisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable database exists in the namespace — the same pattern the BFF already uses for guardrails infrastructure. Because the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop, lifecycle management (cleanup on playground deletion, database pod restarts, credential rotation) gets explicit design attention, and the implementation is designed for portability so it can consume a platform-provided shared connection string instead of self-provisioning once that becomes available in a later release.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Extend the gen-ai BFF so that configuring a playground with RAG creates the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PersistentVolumeClaim, and credentials Secret in the target namespace if no suitable database already exists there (idempotent).\n- Reference the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL 16 image (pgvector included); no custom image build and no build-pipeline onboarding.\n- Provide an injectable connection source so a platform-provided shared database (planned for a later release) can replace self-provisioning by configuration, without a rewrite.\n- Design and implement lifecycle handling: cleanup of database resources on playground deletion, behavior on database pod restart, and credential rotation.\n- Model resource requests/limits on the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL sizing as the starting point; scope provisioning per namespace.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Configuring a playground with RAG in a namespace without an existing database results in a ready PostgreSQL-with-pgvector instance, with no manual user setup.\n- Re-applying the configuration is idempotent: no duplicate database resources are created.\n- Deleting the playground cleans up the provisioned database resources.\n- A database pod restart does not require manual intervention and does not lose the playground's vector data.\n- Credentials can be rotated without a code change, and the rotation procedure is defined in the implementation.\n- The connection source is injectable: switching to a shared platform database connection string is a configuration change, not a code change.\n- Resource requests/limits are set from the model-registry-operator reference sizing and recorded in the implementation.\n- Vector data is isolated per namespace.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- Lifecycle management without a reconciliation loop is the principal risk of this path; the go/no-go decision record should weigh it against the operator path. If it is deemed unmanageable, the recorded last-resort fallback is requiring customers to bring their own PostgreSQL by configuring the OGXServer CR spec with their connection details, aligned with the breaking changes introduced in OGX 1.0.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning): delivered by this path.\n- HLR-4 (separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance): separate database Deployment.\n- HLR-5 (multi-tenant scoping): per-namespace provisioning.\n- HLR-6 (lightweight for resource-constrained environments): reference-based resource sizing.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":89438,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-t