{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9be868f85098caa1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n3\ttitle: 'Go/no-go decision: PostgreSQL provisioning approach for the default vector\n4\t  store'\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n6\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n7\tteam: dashboard-crimson-scrum\n8\ttype: Investigation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tinvestigation_signals:\n11\t  question_specificity: 1\n12\t  source_accessibility: 0\n13\t  local_runnability: 0\n14\t  cluster_hardware_dependence: 0\n15\t  human_judgment_required: -2\n16\timplementation_type: null\n17\tdependencies: []\n18\tai_signals: null\n19\tai_implementability: Low\n20\tai_implementability_score: -1\n21\tjira_key: null\n22\tbranch: null\n23\tgated_by: null\n24\tgate_failure_impact: null\n25\t---\n26\t# Go/No-Go Decision: PostgreSQL Provisioning Approach for the Default Vector Store\n27\t\n28\t## Description\n29\t\n30\tResolves which provisioning approach Gen AI Studio uses to make a PostgreSQL/pgvector instance available out-of-the-box for playground RAG workflows in the target RHOAI release. The primary path is operator-based self-provisioning following the model-registry pattern, which requires the RHOAI-controlled operator layer expected from the dashboard operator modularization effort (RHAISTRAT-7164, in progress). If that operator layer will not be available in time, the fallback is BFF-managed provisioning in the gen-ai BFF; if neither self-provisioning path is viable, customers would bring their own PostgreSQL. The outcome of this investigation selects which provisioning implementation gets built, so it gates all downstream provisioning and validation work.\n31\t\n32\t## Scope\n33\t\n34\t- Define the go/no-go criteria for the decision: the delivery status and forecast of the RHOAI-controlled operator layer from the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164), the release cutoff for the target RHOAI release, and the readiness of each candidate path.\n35\t- Assess each candidate path against the criteria: operator-based self-provisioning, BFF-managed provisioning in the gen-ai BFF, and the bring-your-own-PostgreSQL fallback.\n36\t- Produce a decision record naming the selected path, the criteria used, the conditions under which the bring-your-own-PostgreSQL fallback activates, and the portability requirement (self-provisioning must be replaceable by the platform-provided shared database planned for a later RHOAI release).\n37\t- Present the decision in sprint planning as the go/no-go gate.\n38\t\n39\t## Acceptance Criteria\n40\t\n41\t- A written decision record exists that names the selected provisioning approach for the target release.\n42\t- The record states, for each of the three candidate paths, whether it is selected, deferred, or rejected, with the criterion that drove the outcome.\n43\t- The record defines the conditions under which the bring-your-own-PostgreSQL fallback would be activated instead of self-provisioning.\n44\t- The record identifies the decision date, the release milestone it applies to, and the owner of any re-evaluation.\n45\t- The decision is recorded as an outcome of a sprint-planning session (meeting notes or ticket comment referencing the record).\n46\t\n47\t## Risks / Notes\n48\t\n49\t- The delivery timing of the dashboard operator modularization (RHAISTRAT-7164) is externally controlled by a separate in-progress strategy; this investigation observes that status and decides — it cannot accelerate the underlying work. Late or ambiguous delivery status is an input to the decision, not a blocker to making it.\n50\t\n51\t## HLR Traceability\n52\t\n53\t- HLR-1 (default vector store available out-of-the-box): this investigation determines how out-of-the-box availability is achieved and which provisioning implementation is built.\n54\t- Strategy open question (scoping model): per-namespace scoping confirmed for Gen AI Studio now that Auto Rag is decoupled; recorded in the decision record.\n55\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a8672a41cf9d577d","agent_id":"a4b2e92ad49986c18","session_id":"90eb4e85-7690-4532-889d-850d91d0a9f8","uuid":"03558914-3836-463f-8190-0daaed73e31c","timestamp":"2026-10-25T20:41:52.522Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9be868f85098caa1","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
