{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-ae2ca09f32889967","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-E002\n3\ttitle: Configure pgvector as the remote vector_io provider for Gen AI Studio playground\n4\t  RAG\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n6\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n7\tteam: dashboard-crimson-scrum\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 1\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n14\t  existing_foundation: 1\n15\t  open_questions: 1\n16\t  external_dependency: 1\n17\t  human_process_gates: 1\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: 1\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tdependencies: []\n22\tinvestigation_signals: null\n23\tai_implementability: High\n24\tai_implementability_score: 9\n25\tjira_key: null\n26\tbranch: null\n27\tgated_by: null\n28\tgate_failure_impact: null\n29\t---\n30\t# Configure pgvector as the Remote Vector_io Provider for Gen AI Studio Playground RAG\n31\t\n32\t## Description\n33\t\n34\tDelivers the vector database integration inside Gen AI Studio: when the gen-ai BFF creates an OGXServer instance for a playground with RAG enabled, it configures OGX's remote pgvector vector_io provider (activated via the ENABLE_PGVECTOR environment guard), reusing the existing remote vector store implementation. The vector database runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX server. OGX's application storage backend is a separate, independent configuration and remains on its existing default — this change does not touch it, and there is no SQLite-to-PostgreSQL migration concern.\n35\t\n36\t## Scope\n37\t\n38\t- Extend the gen-ai BFF's OGXServer instance creation for playgrounds so that, when RAG is enabled, the vector_io provider is set to the remote pgvector provider instead of relying on the inline provider bundled in the distribution image.\n39\t- Consume the PostgreSQL connection details for the provider from an injectable source (Kubernetes Secret / connection string), designed so the source is swappable: the self-provisioned per-namespace instance today, the platform-provided shared database planned for a later release, or a user-provided external vector database.\n40\t- Scope the vector store per namespace for multi-tenant isolation.\n41\t- Reuse and adapt the existing remote vector store provider implementation rather than writing a new provider.\n42\t- Leave the OGX application storage backend (kv/sql) configuration unchanged from its current default.\n43\t- Keep the configuration additive and revertible: restoring the prior provider configuration returns the playground to its previous behavior.\n44\t\n45\t## Acceptance Criteria\n46\t\n47\t- A playground created with RAG enabled gets an OGXServer instance whose vector_io provider is the remote pgvector provider, and the vector database it talks to runs as a separate process from the OGX server.\n48\t- The provider registers successfully and a basic vector insert/query round-trip works through the remote pgvector provider from the playground.\n49\t- The OGX application storage backend configuration emitted by the BFF is unchanged from its current default.\n50\t- Vector stores are scoped per namespace: a playground in one namespace cannot read or write vector data of a playground in another namespace.\n51\t- The connection source is injectable: pointing the provider at a different connection string (an external database or a shared platform database) requires a configuration change only, no code change.\n52\t- Reverting the configuration change restores the previous vector_io provider behavior.\n53\t\n54\t## HLR Traceability\n55\t\n56\t- HLR-2 (playground RAG workflows function with the default vector store, no loss of capability): this epic delivers the provider wiring those workflows run on.\n57\t- HLR-4 (default vector store runs as a separate process, decoupled from the OGX/Llama Stack instance): delivered by configuring the remote provider.\n58\t- HLR-1 (out-of-the-box availability): partially covered — the provider wiring half; the database provisioning half is delivered by the selected provisioning implementation.\n59\t- HLR-5 (multi-tenant scoping): per-namespace provider scoping delivered here and verified end-to-end during validation.\n60\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a8672a41cf9d577d","agent_id":"a4b2e92ad49986c18","session_id":"90eb4e85-7690-4532-889d-850d91d0a9f8","uuid":"1e385e3d-89eb-408d-83f6-e4dd7dc1043b","timestamp":"2026-10-25T20:41:53.143Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-ae2ca09f32889967","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
