{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a42e23872add278c","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8004-BRANCH-B-E004\n3\ttitle: BFF-managed PostgreSQL/pgvector provisioning for Gen AI Studio playground (fallback\n4\t  path)\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8004\n6\tcomponent: Gen AI Studio\n7\tteam: dashboard-crimson-scrum\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tbranch: B\n11\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n12\tdependencies:\n13\t- RHAISTRAT-8004-E001\n14\tgate_failure_impact:\n15\t  action: remove\n16\t  fallback_approach: If the go/no-go decision selects the operator path, this epic\n17\t    is not built; if BFF-managed provisioning is deemed unmanageable, require customers\n18\t    to bring their own PostgreSQL via user-configured OGXServer CR connection details\n19\t    (aligned with OGX 1.0 breaking changes).\n20\tai_signals:\n21\t  change_specificity: 1\n22\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n23\t  adapter_pattern: 1\n24\t  existing_foundation: 1\n25\t  open_questions: 0\n26\t  external_dependency: 1\n27\t  human_process_gates: 1\n28\t  repo_access: 1\n29\t  architecture_claims: 1\n30\timplementation_type: null\n31\tinvestigation_signals: null\n32\tai_implementability: High\n33\tai_implementability_score: 8\n34\tjira_key: null\n35\t---\n36\t# BFF-Managed PostgreSQL/pgvector Provisioning for Gen AI Studio Playground (Fallback Path)\n37\t\n38\t## Description\n39\t\n40\tImplemented if the go/no-go decision selects the BFF path because the RHOAI-controlled operator layer will not be available in time for the target release: the gen-ai BFF provisions the PostgreSQL/pgvector instance directly when a playground with RAG is configured and no suitable database exists in the namespace — the same pattern the BFF already uses for guardrails infrastructure. Because the BFF is not an operator with a reconciliation loop, lifecycle management (cleanup on playground deletion, database pod restarts, credential rotation) gets explicit design attention, and the implementation is designed for portability so it can consume a platform-provided shared connection string instead of self-provisioning once that becomes available in a later release.\n41\t\n42\t## Scope\n43\t\n44\t- Extend the gen-ai BFF so that configuring a playground with RAG creates the PostgreSQL Deployment, Service, PersistentVolumeClaim, and credentials Secret in the target namespace if no suitable database already exists there (idempotent).\n45\t- Reference the existing Red Hat PostgreSQL 16 image (pgvector included); no custom image build and no build-pipeline onboarding.\n46\t- Provide an injectable connection source so a platform-provided shared database (planned for a later release) can replace self-provisioning by configuration, without a rewrite.\n47\t- Design and implement lifecycle handling: cleanup of database resources on playground deletion, behavior on database pod restart, and credential rotation.\n48\t- Model resource requests/limits on the model-registry-operator's PostgreSQL sizing as the starting point; scope provisioning per namespace.\n49\t\n50\t## Acceptance Criteria\n51\t\n52\t- Configuring a playground with RAG in a namespace without an existing database results in a ready PostgreSQL-with-pgvector instance, with no manual user setup.\n53\t- Re-applying the configuration is idempotent: no duplicate database resources are created.\n54\t- Deleting the playground cleans up the provisioned database resources.\n55\t- A database pod restart does not require manual intervention and does not lose the playground's vector data.\n56\t- Credentials can be rotated without a code change, and the rotation procedure is defined in the implementation.\n57\t- The connection source is injectable: switching to a shared platform database connection string is a configuration change, not a code change.\n58\t- Resource requests/limits are set from the model-registry-operator reference sizing and recorded in the implementation.\n59\t- Vector data is isolated per namespace.\n60\t\n61\t## Risks / Notes\n62\t\n63\t- Lifecycle management without a reconciliation loop is the principal risk of this path; the go/no-go decision record should weigh it against the operator path. If it is deemed unmanageable, the recorded last-resort fallback is requiring customers to bring their own PostgreSQL by configuring the OGXServer CR spec with their connection details, aligned with the breaking changes introduced in OGX 1.0.\n64\t\n65\t## HLR Traceability\n66\t\n67\t- HLR-1 (default vector store available out-of-the-box, no manual provisioning): delivered by this path.\n68\t- HLR-4 (separate process, decoupled from the OGX instance): separate database Deployment.\n69\t- HLR-5 (multi-tenant scoping): per-namespace provisioning.\n70\t- HLR-6 (lightweight for resource-constrained environments): reference-based resource sizing.\n71\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a8672a41cf9d577d","agent_id":"a4b2e92ad49986c18","session_id":"90eb4e85-7690-4532-889d-850d91d0a9f8","uuid":"ecf7813b-ef3b-4623-b7c0-90cdd1d2c807","timestamp":"2026-10-25T20:41:57.664Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a42e23872add278c","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8004 decomposition"}
