{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a6a58a3abd58681d","type":"tool_result","content":"**Short**: OGX distribution is a Red Hat-customized build of the OGX (formerly llama-stack) AI agent gateway that exposes multi-provider inference, RAG, and agentic APIs over HTTP on port 8321. [source: distribution/config.yaml:257, distribution/entrypoint.sh:79]\n\n**Detailed**: ogx-distribution packages the OGX framework as a containerized Python service for RHOAI. It acts as a unified AI agent gateway that routes inference requests to configurable remote providers (vLLM, AWS Bedrock, IBM WatsonX, Azure, Vertex AI, OpenAI, Gemini, Anthropic), provides vector I/O through Milvus, PGVector, or Qdrant backends, and offers tool runtime capabilities including web search and file operations. The distribution uses PostgreSQL for both key-value and SQL-backed persistence of agent state, conversations, batches, and metadata. Authentication is handled via OAuth2 token validation with JWKS when configured. The container image is built on an AIPCC base image with Python dependencies installed from the RHEL AI Python Package Index via Hermeto-prefetched packages, and ships pre-fetched Docling and embedding model artifacts. [source: distribution/config.yaml:1-280, Dockerfile.konflux:1-58, distribution/entrypoint.sh:1-79]\n\n## Architectural Analysis\n--\n|------|--------|-------------|------|----------|------------|------|\n| 1 | Client | OGX Server | 8321/TCP | HTTP | Platform-delegated | OAuth2 bearer token (when configured) |\n| 2 | OGX Server | Vector DB (Milvus / PGVector / Qdrant) | Configurable | HTTP/gRPC/SQL | Configurable (mTLS for Milvus) | Token / API key / password |\n| 3 | OGX Server | Inference Provider | Configurable | HTTP/HTTPS | TLS | API token |\n| 4 | OGX Server | PostgreSQL | 5432/TCP | PostgreSQL | Platform-delegated | Username/password |\n--\n| Anthropic | HTTP client | Optional | 443/TCP | HTTPS | TLS | Cloud inference via Anthropic API |\n| Milvus | HTTP client | Optional | Configurable | HTTP/HTTPS | mTLS configurable | Remote vector database for RAG |\n| PGVector | SQL client | Optional | 5432/TCP | PostgreSQL | Configurable | Vector database via PostgreSQL extension |\n| Qdrant | HTTP/gRPC client | Optional | 6333/TCP (HTTP), 6334/TCP (gRPC) | HTTP or gRPC | HTTPS configurable | Vector database for RAG |\n| Brave Search | HTTP client | Optional | 443/TCP | HTTPS | TLS | Web search tool runtime |\n---- model-registry ----\n## Purpose\n\n**Short**: Kubernetes operator that manages the lifecycle of ModelRegistry, Catalog, and AIHub custom resources, provisioning model-registry service instances with kube-rbac-proxy authentication, PostgreSQL storage, and Gateway API or OpenShift Route ingress. [source: cmd/modelregistry.go:169, internal/controller/modelregistry_controller.go:282, internal/controller/modelcatalog_controller.go:183]\n\n**Detailed**: The model-registry-operator is a controller-runtime-based Kubernetes operator that reconciles three CRDs — ModelRegistry (v1alpha1/v1beta1), Catalog (v1alpha1), and AIHub (v1alpha1) — to provision and manage model registry service instances within the RHOAI platform. For each ModelRegistry CR, the operator creates a Deployment running the model-registry proxy backed by either PostgreSQL or MySQL storage, with an optional kube-rbac-proxy sidecar that enforces SubjectAccessReview-based authorization on the HTTPS port. The operator manages the full resource lifecycle including Services, ServiceAccounts, RBAC Roles/Bindings, Secrets, NetworkPolicies, and ingress resources (Gateway API HTTPRoutes or OpenShift Routes depending on cluster capabilities). The ModelCatalog reconciler similarly provisions a centralized catalog service with its own PostgreSQL instance. The operator detects cluster capabilities at startup — including OpenShift APIs, BYOIDC mode (where user.openshift.io is absent), Gateway API availability, and the platform Auth CRD — to conditionally create platform-specific resources. [source: internal/controller/util.go:36-39, internal/controller/config/defaults.go:197-211, internal/controller/config/templates/deployment.yaml.tmpl:220-272]\n\n## Architectural Analysis\n\nThe model-registry-operator follows a multi-controller pattern with two primary reconcilers — `ModelRegistryReconciler` and `ModelCatalogReconciler` — sharing common resource-management utilities for proxy configuration, RBAC, and ingress. Both controllers use Go templates embedded in `internal/controller/config/templates/` to render Kubernetes manifests, enabling a declarative approach to managed workload configuration.\n--\n| Dockerfile:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/manager\"] |\n| model-catalog | Controller-created Deployment | catalog ({image}), kube-rbac-proxy ({image}) |\n| model-catalog-postgres | Controller-created Deployment | postgresql ({image}) |\n| model-registry-operator | CLI Tool, Go executable | Cobra CLI application; Go entrypoint |\n| model-registry-operator-controller-manager | Deployment | manager (controller) |\n| {registry-name} | Controller-created Deployment | rest-container ({image}), kube-rbac-proxy ({image}) |\n| {registry-name}-postgres | Controller-created Deployment | postgres ({image}) |\n\n## APIs Exposed\n\n### Custom Resource Definitions (CRDs)\n--\n| Service Name | Type | Port | Target Port | Protocol | Encryption | Auth | Exposure |\n|------------|----|----|-----------|--------|----------|----|--------|\n| model-catalog-postgres | ClusterIP | 5432/TCP | 5432 | PostgreSQL | Unknown | Unknown | Internal |\n| model-catalog | ClusterIP | 8080/TCP | 8080 | HTTP | Unknown | Unknown | Internal |\n| model-catalog | ClusterIP | 8443/TCP | 8443 | HTTPS | Unknown | Unknown | Internal |\n| model-registry-operator-controller-manager-metrics-service | ClusterIP | 8443/TCP | https | TCP | Unknown | Unknown | Internal |\n| {registry-name}-postgres | ClusterIP | 5432/TCP | 5432 | PostgreSQL | Unknown | Unknown | Internal |\n| {registry-name} | ClusterIP | 8080/TCP | 8080 | HTTP | Unknown | Unknown | Internal |\n| {registry-name} | ClusterIP | 8443/TCP | 8443 | HTTPS | Unknown | Unknown | Internal |\n\n### Ingress\n--\n|-----------|----|-------|--------------|-----------|\n| model-catalog-kube-rbac-proxy | kubernetes.io/tls | model-catalog | controller template | Unknown |\n| model-catalog-postgres | Opaque | model-catalog/catalog, model-catalog-postgres/postgresql | controller template | Unknown |\n| {registry-name}-kube-rbac-proxy | kubernetes.io/tls | {registry-name} | controller template | Unknown |\n| {registry-name}-postgres-credentials | Opaque | {registry-name}-postgres/postgres | controller Go source | Unknown |\n| {template-value} | referenced | {registry-name}/rest-container, {registry-name} | controller template | Unknown |\n---- odh-dashboard gen-ai ----\n\n**Detailed**: odh-dashboard is a multi-component platform console consisting of three tiers: (1) a React/PatternFly single-page application served via Webpack Module Federation that hosts domain-specific UI modules; (2) a Node.js Fastify backend that proxies Kubernetes API requests using user impersonation and serves static assets behind kube-rbac-proxy; and (3) a Go operator (dashboard-operator) built on controller-runtime v0.23.3 that reconciles Dashboard custom resources, managing the lifecycle of the console deployment including RBAC, routes, and sidecar BFF services. The federated architecture includes ten Go BFF (backend-for-frontend) sidecars — core-bff, gen-ai, model-registry, mlflow, eval-hub, maas, agent-ops, automl, autorag, and data-registry — each serving domain-specific API endpoints on dedicated TLS-encrypted ports with Bearer token authentication. The operator watches platform CRDs (DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, KServe InferenceService, ModelRegistry, MLflow, TrustyAI, Feast) to reflect platform state in the dashboard and manages Gateway API HTTPRoutes for request routing. A bundled Workspace controller reconciles kubeflow.org/v1beta1 Workspace and WorkspaceKind resources with validating and conversion webhooks. [source: Dockerfile.konflux:52, Dockerfile.konflux.dashboard-operator:37, dashboard-operator/charts/dashboard/values.yaml:15-18, manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml:15, dashboard-operator/go.mod:6-13]\n\n## Architectural Analysis\n\nThe odh-dashboard follows a micro-frontend architecture using Webpack Module Federation, where a host React application dynamically loads domain-specific UI modules (gen-ai, model-registry, notebooks, eval-hub, agent-ops, automl, autorag, data-registry, maas, mlflow) at runtime via `/_mf/:name/*` paths. Each UI module is paired with a dedicated Go BFF sidecar that handles its backend operations, creating a vertically-sliced architecture where each domain owns its full request path from UI through BFF to Kubernetes API. The federation configuration (in `manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml`) maps path prefixes to sidecar ports, enabling a single ingress point to fan out to domain-specific backends. [source: manifests/rhoai/base/federation-configmap.yaml:15, backend/src/routes/module-federation.ts:1]\n\nThe dashboard-operator reconciles Dashboard custom resources created by the rhods-operator, deploying and configuring the console pod alongside its BFF sidecars. The operator implements a declarative ownership model where the parent Dashboard CR controls the lifecycle of Deployments, Services, Routes, NetworkPolicies, PodDisruptionBudgets, and RBAC resources via controller-runtime's `Owns()` watches. Controller watches on platform CRDs (DataScienceCluster, DSCInitialization, KServe InferenceService, ModelRegistry, MLflow, TrustyAI, Feast) enable the dashboard to reflect the real-time state of the AI platform without polling. The operator uses `odh-platform-utilities` for platform detection, manifest rendering, and deployment helpers. [source: dashboard-operator/charts/dashboard/values.yaml:15-18, dashboard-operator/go.mod:6, dashboard-operator/config/rbac/role.yaml:1]\n\n--\n| frontend (host app) | React/TypeScript SPA | Workspace frontend and module federation host |\n| gen-ai BFF | Go BFF Sidecar | Federated gen-ai backend-for-frontend service |\n| k8s-core | TypeScript Library | Shared dashboard k8s-core workspace |\n| maas BFF | Go BFF Sidecar | Federated maas backend-for-frontend service |\n| manager | Go controller-runtime operator, Go executable | Executable Go command selected by the runtime image build; cmd/manager |\n--\n| packages/eval-hub/Dockerfile:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n| packages/gen-ai/Dockerfile.workspace:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n| packages/gen-ai/Dockerfile:CMD | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n| packages/gen-ai/bff/Dockerfile:CMD | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n| packages/maas/Dockerfile.workspace:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n| packages/maas/Dockerfile:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n| packages/mlflow/Dockerfile.workspace:ENTRYPOINT | Container entrypoint | [\"/bff\"] |\n--\n| /eval-hub/api/v1/* | ALL | 8543 | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | user_token |  | Eval-Hub BFF API |\n| /gen-ai/api/* | ALL | 8143 | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | user_token |  | Gen-Ai BFF API |\n| /gen-ai/api/v1/* | ALL | 8143 | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | user_token |  | Gen-Ai BFF API |\n| /healthcheck | GET | 8080 | HTTP | Unknown | None | None |  | Core BFF health check |\n| /healthz | GET | 8081 | HTTP |  | Unknown | Unknown |  | httpGet probe |\n| /maas/api/* | ALL | 8243 | HTTPS | Unknown | TLS | user_token |  | Maas BFF API |\n--\n| odh-dashboard-operator-role | components.platform.opendatahub.io | dashboards/finalizers | update |\n| odh-dashboard-operator-role |  | configmaps, persistentvolumeclaims, secrets, serviceaccounts, services | create, delete, get, list, patch, update, watch |\n| odh-dashboard-operator-role |  | namespaces | get, list, patch, wat